7You Should Write A Book! True Tales of An Unstable Life"M
KKim Cosier, Rachel Harper, Jeff Sapp, Jody Sokolower, Melissa Bollow Tempel"â
ÜThe games are about to begin.
Au pair and Canadian ex-pat Isabelle Beausejour has been living abroad for most of her twenty-four years, traveling the world with her irresponsible father. When Isabelle finds herself stranded in Bangkok, with no job, no money, and nowhere to turn, she soon becomes desperate.
Canadian Security Intelligence Service officer Garrett Downing is on the hunt for Canadian military goods that have gone missing. Instead, he finds himself coming to the aid of a young Canadian woman with more resourcefulness than common sense.
Isabelle has no choice but to accept a stranger's help in getting back home to Canada. Once there, however, as enemies turn into lovers, it soon becomes a game of keeping secrets. Garrett is more than he seems. Isabelle knows more than she's willing to admit. Will she choose loyalty to her father over the love of a man who tells lies for a living?"í
èAlice had a secret. At just seventeen she had found and fallen deeply in love with the perfect man. The kind of man you don't ever forget, the kind of sexual experience you can never replicate, but that man had been her teacher.
Fourteen Years later - married and a mother - Alice reluctantly attends her high school reunion and although bringing back bitter sweet memories of her clandestine adolescence, she leave the party with a gentle closure on the part of her past that she couldn't, until now, put to rest.
On the short car ride home, stopping briefly for traffic she glances behind her.
There, through the dark windscreen, dim shadows and the sudden shroud of her unexpected tears she's met with the face of the man that had filled her dreams for over a decade...
Tom Chambers.
He had taught her how to love. Both inspired aand pushed her fledgling sexual journey to its youthful limits and made her beautiful over all others. He was temptation itself, and he was here.
On the night she had decided to let it all go, to let him go...he was refusing to let her.
'The beautiful and unapologetic honesty of true love, raw sexual ownership and internal struggle - are all emotionally alive here. Delicately dissecting that hidden, duplicitous part within us all that we're never fully comfortable to recognise or accept.'
Please note ** This is the concluding part of a story about Tom and Alice that started in 'The Not Gate' by Tammy Bench. We recommend reading 'The Not Gate' first, but this can also be read as a stand alone book if you wish."¬	
ø	Years ago, drowning her sorrows in a giant bowl of chips and guacamole, Kristan shared yet another story of falling down on the job (no no - that's not a figure of speech) with her college roommate, Jen, who couldn't believe the absurd luck of this person sitting across from her.
"I hope you're writing all this down. You should write a book about all this stuff,[?] Jen announced.
In this collection of true tales (at least 87% of which have been confirmed to be true), you'll meet Kristan's quirky parents, you'll get a glimpse into the life of a person potentially suffering borderline personality disorder(s), and you'll even get a peek right in the birthing room for the delivery of her first-born child.
In You Should Write A Book!, Kristan shares her missteps in life with the same honest and hilarious vulnerability she uses in her popular* blog, Bring Mommy A Martini.
*This is the only lie in the book**. Her blog is only marginally popular, being read regularly only by members of her neighborhood Facebook group, but even that is doubtful.
**This is a complete fabrication. This is only one in a whole handful of lies within the book. But aside from those four or five things, the rest is totally true."ƒ
¡There once was
a boy,
a rabbit,
a magic seed,
and a book.
The boy, who was bored,
went looking for something to do.
The rabbit, who was curious,
explored until he was lost.
The seed was thirsty,
but soon it grew and grew and grew.
And the book?
You are holding it in your hands.
Turn the pages!
It will make you
so happy."’
“There has never been a more important time for students to understand sexism, gender, and sexuality--or to make schools nurturing places for all of us. The thought-provoking articles and curriculum in this life-changing book will be invaluable to everyone who wants to address these issues in their classroom, school, home, and community."Ÿ
÷While Slipper is a small little cat, new to the world, she is not afraid of exploring! Still, this new house feels big and lonely, and Slipper feels a bit scared. She needs to muster up the courage to go outside and speak to someone, and befriend someone as well. How will Slipper get over her fear of this new place and make friends?
"Slipper has a New Home" is the first in a series of Slipper and Friends books by Tess Rixen, a thirteen-year-old author who devotes most of her time to writing, and only writing. Here, Slipper tells of the hard transition of moving from an old home into a new one. As she transfers to not only a new home, but a new owner, Slipper is connected to human feelings of this situation in real life. Most young kids will feel sad, confused and/or scared in this kind of scenario. Leaving old friends, meeting new people around the neighborhood, adjusting to a new place and creating new friendships is not an easy task.
Parents, if you're looking for a book to help your child cope with moving, this cute, beautifully illustrated little book will do just that.
